{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-121756",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14269128/assets/3ca3fa1fe2759b972b9056c8a0b6c0f9a50058517a169e24c9a1999005719b2d.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the balloon's height, and $z$ is the distance between the child and the balloon. The quantity $z=120$ centimeters does not change with time because the string's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{120} \\\\ y & =120\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(120\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(120\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConverting the given information from degrees to radians, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{180}^{∘}}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =(120\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{60} & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{60}$ radians per second.",
